FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/analyzer.rs:468 — bare `catch` body is never analyzed when `catch_var` is `None` — `if let Some(var)` skips `analyze_body(catch_body)` entirely, while `src/interpreter.rs:1636` (`is_some() || !catch_body.is_empty()`) and `src/bytecode/codegen.rs:536` and `src/linter.rs:320-324` all treat `None` plus a non-empty body as a real handler — an undefined name inside a bare `catch` slips past analysis and surfaces as a runtime failure instead of a spanned `AnalyzerError`, and no `edge_*` test pins it — analyze `catch_body` in a pushed scope even when `catch_var` is `None`, declaring only when `Some`.
2. [MAJOR] docs/GRAMMAR.md:321 — `try_statement` writes `{ 'catch' [ identifier [ identifier ] ] { statement } }` with repetition braces, admitting zero-or-more catches — `SPEC.md:842` says a `try` has one `catch` and `src/parser.rs:1620` implements a single `catch_var`/`catch_body`, so a second `catch` falls into the first catch-body loop and mis-parses instead of being refused — change the outer `{...}` to `[...]` for an optional single catch and add `edge_a_second_catch_is_refused`.
